  "query_preview": ".. Monsoonal moisture return continues across the \nsouthwest as ridging remains overhead. Beneath this ridge, PWATs in \nexcess of 1\" continue to push north across most valley locations \nwith locations across southern UT potentially exceeding 1.6\" PWATs \nonce again this afternoon. Daily chances for showers, thunderstorms, \nand potentially flash flooding continue through at least Thursday \nacross southern and central UT with this pattern in place. Modest \nmoisture return continues to push across northern UT, though most \nstorms have remained anchored to the terrain further east each \nafternoon due to drier PBL conditions across northwest UT valleys \nand considerably weaker steering flow. This brings great \nuncertainty to the forecast through Tuesday morning, keeping PoPs \nsomewhat lower across most of the Wasatch Front and northwest UT \ncompared to higher elevation locations. Deeper moisture looks to \nadvect further north as some stronger synoptic forcing moves in \nfrom the west, increasing PoPs starting Tuesday afternoon with the\nhighest confidence of heavy rainfall and Thunderstorms occurring \nWednesday afternoon. \n\nThunderstorm development across central and southern UT will be \nsomewhat delayed closer to 3pm today due to an early shortwave \ntraversing the region, leaving some localized subsidence in its \nwake. PWAT anomalies across southern and central UT once again range \nfrom 0.5-1\" of water as monsoonal moisture lingers this afternoon. \nCAPE profiles potentially in excess of 1000J/kg with deep warm cloud \nlayers up to 3km continue to support the development of efficient \nrainmakers with heavy rain expected from well established storms. \nThe stronger instability axis with CAPE values >= 1000J/kg once \nagain resides across southwestern UT, supported by both SPC \nMesoanalysis and current CAM guidance, with storms expected to \ninitiate along higher terrain and propagate down terrain toward the \ninstability gradients. \n\nAs the ridge builds overhead, temperatures on Monday are forecast to \nreach into the upper 90s to low 100s across most valley locations \nacross northwest UT. This has resulted in widespread \"major\" \nHeatRisk values, primarily within valley locations along the I-15 \ncorridor and Great Salt Lake Desert, with a few areas reaching \n\"major\". Overnight relief following warmer temperatures today are \nforecast to increase the threat with overnight lows tonight \ngenerally ranging from the upper 60s to mid 70s. As such, a heat \nadvisory has been issued for the aforementioned locations. \n\nForecast guidance continues to bring daily chances of rain across \nmuch of UT through at least Friday, though guidance is now in better \nagreement with drier mid-level air pushing into the Great Basin as \nwe head into the weekend. This dry punch is associated with a mid-\nlevel trough that is forecast to impact the pacific northwest later \nthis week bringing prolonged dry westerly flow through the end of \nthe forecast period. This will serve to greatly limit precipitation \nchances across the forecast area as we enter the weekend, keeping \nany chances more than likely confined to higher terrain.\n\nLastly, some severe potential exists on Wednesday across northern UT \nand southwest WY as a shortwave trough traverses the Great Basin in \nthe afternoon hours. Confidence regarding timing and location of \nthis shortwave remain highly uncertain at this time, though the \nbroad synoptic pattern associated with this would support a \npotential damaging wind threat if it were to materialize.",
